{"images":[],"manifest":{"budgetTokens":60000,"countMethod":"proxy","model":"gpt-6-luna","packSha":"d8da94a8c4fede535f8dea21e26aa13b19a56fb1d7fad353691878d40935ab58","packTokens":16444,"recipeVersion":"1","redactions":0,"sections":[{"id":"tpl","tokens":1286,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"tpl-end","tokens":49,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"test-kit","tokens":2052,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"approved-screens-rules","tokens":217,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"contract","tokens":117,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"acs","tokens":4518,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"approved-screens","tokens":2186,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"stubs","tokens":275,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"previous-tests","tokens":2004,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"pointers","tokens":32,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"failures","tokens":3277,"trimmed":false,"trust":"derived"},{"id":"task","tokens":299,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"},{"id":"recap","tokens":35,"trimmed":false,"trust":"trusted"}],"stage":"author-tests"},"pointers":[{"path":"contracts/openapi.yaml","reason":"the locked API contract; the client and test handlers in lib/api are generated from it"}],"system":"You write black-box acceptance tests for a TypeScript web app (React, with the factory's component kit), one test per acceptance criterion, before the feature exists.\nRules:\n- Put the tests in tests/ at the repo root, as *.test.ts files (*.test.tsx for a screen test), with vitest (import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\"). The \"@/\" import alias points at the app's source root.\n- Test through public surfaces only: exported functions and modules, route handlers, and a screen as a person uses it.\n- Name each acceptance test exactly AC_<req>_<n>_<Words> (the it() title) for acceptance criterion AC-<req>.<n>, e.g. it(\"AC_1_2_ReturnsNotFoundWhenOrderMissing\", ...). Name characterisation tests CHAR_<Words>. The factory finds tests by these titles.\n- New APIs exist as stubs that throw new Error(\"not implemented\"); tests must import them and fail for now.\n- Also write characterisation tests for existing behaviour next to the change that must NOT change; those must pass today. A new app has little of its own: write none when there is nothing to keep.\n- Don't change production code, package.json, tsconfig or the vitest config.\n- You may run \"npx tsc --noEmit\" to check the tests compile. When every test is written, run your own new test files once (npx vitest run <your files>) and read how each test fails: on an assertion or \"not implemented\", never on a crash in the set-up or while rendering. Fix a test that crashes, or that passes, before you return. Run nothing else, and at most twice: the factory runs and judges every test itself in its test lab.\n- Test each criterion at its level: \"unit\" criteria call the module directly; this web app has no API of its own: the API is a separate service with locked tests of its own, and here it is reached only through the generated client. An \"api\" or \"job\" criterion is tested as what this app does with the API's answer: answer the call with the contract's test handlers or a mocked client, then assert what the screen or the web code does. Never test a rule only the server can keep (what is stored, what survives a restart, seeded records, the order a status may change in, what bad data is answered with) by calling the generated client and expecting the rule to hold: nothing in this repo may implement it. Never test the generated client itself. Skip \"manual\" criteria: a person checks those.\n- Outside systems (an email or SMS provider, a payment service, another company's API, a file store) are never called by a test: the test lab has no network. Put a stand-in behind the seam the code reaches the system through: replace the system's module with vi.mock. The stand-in records every call it gets; assert from that record what was sent (to whom, with which values, how many times), and make the stand-in fail where a criterion is about the system failing. Use a stand-in the tests already have for that system before you write one. A stand-in is test code: keep it with the tests, where it is locked with them.\n- A \"ui\" criterion gets a screen test: a tests/*.test.tsx file whose FIRST line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\". Render the screen's container (components/screens/<s-id>/container.tsx, the page with its data and behaviour) with render() from \"@testing-library/react\", and act as a person does with userEvent from \"@testing-library/user-event\".\n- Assert what a person sees and can do: find things by role and name (screen.getByRole(\"button\", { name: \"Save\" })), by label (getByLabelText) or by the words on the page, never by class name, test id or component internals. Use findBy... for what appears after data loads.\n- There is no router, no server and no network in a screen test. Navigation is recorded: import { nav, at } from \"./support/screen\" (from tests/), call at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }) before render() to set the address, and assert where the page went with nav.went (e.g. expect(nav.went).toContain(\"/orders/7\")).\n- Stand in for the API with vi.stubGlobal(\"fetch\", ...) or by mocking the generated client module (vi.mock), and answer with data in the shape the contract gives. Give each test its own answers; undo them in afterEach (vi.unstubAllGlobals(), vi.restoreAllMocks()).\n- A screen test proves behaviour on the screen: what is shown for which data, what a click or a typed value does, which message appears. It cannot judge looks (colour, spacing, a width): don't assert styles or class names.\n- Don't edit tests/support/ or the vitest config; the factory owns them. Don't use snapshots.\n\n\n- Give no HTTP probes: the endpoints are served by the API, not by this app. Return an empty list of probes.\nReturn the list of tests you wrote (acId, file, method name).\n\nThe approved-screens section lists the screens a person approved for these requirements: route, states, and the exact words on them (title, buttons, field labels, column headers, empty, error, success and validation messages, toasts; \"change\" for a design note). Where a criterion is about what the user sees or is told, take the expected values from there, word for word, and do not invent other wording. A criterion with no screen there is tested as before. For each test that opens one of the approved screens, also return that screen's id as \"screen\" (the screen the test renders, which can be another than the one its requirement is listed under): the test is then held at the task that builds that screen. Leave it out for a test that opens no screen.\n\nAPI CONTRACT (locked: contracts/openapi.yaml). The app talks to the API only through the generated client in lib/api/client.ts; never write fetch calls or response types by hand, and never edit lib/api or contracts/openapi.yaml. Tests that need the API start the generated handlers from lib/api/client.msw.ts with msw's setupServer (msw/node); they answer with the contract's examples, so assert on that data.","tools":[],"user":"What is already in this checkout for your tests, so you need not search for it:\n\ntests/support/screen.ts (as it is; import from it, don't copy or change it):\n// Written by ai-factory from ai-factory design 20261014-hackathon-problem-statement-fe-ae5c v1 (7e33e7a210ad), approved by Ahsan (via web) on 2026-10-14.\n// What a screen test reads and sets about the address. In tests the app's navigation (lib/nav) is replaced by this:\n// nothing really navigates, and every place the page went to is kept in nav.went.\nexport const nav = { path: \"/\", params: {} as Record<string, string>, went: [] as string[] };\n\n/** Put the test at an address before rendering, e.g. at(\"/orders/7\", { tab: \"paid\" }). */\nexport function at(path: string, params: Record<string, string> = {}): void {\n  nav.path = path;\n  nav.params = params;\n}\n\nexport function resetNav(): void {\n  nav.path = \"/\";\n  nav.params = {};\n  nav.went = [];\n}\n\ntests/support/setup.ts (as it is; import from it, don't copy or change it):\n// Written by ai-factory from ai-factory design 20261014-hackathon-problem-statement-fe-ae5c v1 (7e33e7a210ad), approved by Ahsan (via web) on 2026-10-14.\n// Runs before every test file. A screen test (a *.test.tsx file whose first line is \"// @vitest-environment jsdom\") renders\n// a page with no router and no browser: navigation is recorded in ./screen, and the few browser parts jsdom lacks are\n// filled in. A plain *.test.ts file is not affected.\nimport { afterEach, vi } from \"vitest\";\nimport { resetNav } from \"./screen\";\n\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/nav\", async () => {\n  const { createElement } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  return {\n    useNav: () => ({ path: nav.path, go: (to: string) => { nav.went.push(to); }, param: (name: string) => nav.params[name] ?? null }),\n    Link: ({ to, onClick, ...props }: { to: string; onClick?: (e: unknown) => void } & Record<string, unknown>) =>\n      createElement(\"a\", { ...props, href: to, onClick: (e: { preventDefault(): void }) => { onClick?.(e); e.preventDefault(); nav.went.push(to); } }),\n  };\n});\n\n// A page that uses the router's package itself, not lib/nav, gets the same stand-in: outside the app it has no router.\nvi.mock(\"next/navigation\", async (original) => {\n  const real = await original<typeof import(\"next/navigation\")>();\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  const go = (to: string) => { nav.went.push(to); };\n  return {\n    ...real,\n    useRouter: () => ({ push: go, replace: go, back: () => {}, forward: () => {}, refresh: () => {}, prefetch: () => {} }),\n    usePathname: () => nav.path,\n    useSearchParams: () => new URLSearchParams(nav.params),\n    useParams: () => nav.params,\n  };\n});\n\nvi.mock(\"next/link\", async () => {\n  const { createElement } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { nav } = await import(\"./screen\");\n  return {\n    default: ({ href, onClick, prefetch: _p, replace: _r, scroll: _s, ...props }: { href: unknown; onClick?: (e: unknown) => void } & Record<string, unknown>) =>\n      createElement(\"a\", { ...props, href: String(href), onClick: (e: { preventDefault(): void }) => { onClick?.(e); e.preventDefault(); nav.went.push(String(href)); } }),\n  };\n});\n\n// A page is tested without the app's frame, so render() puts around it what the frame gives every page: the tooltip\n// provider (a page with a tooltip cannot render without it) and the place toasts appear in.\nvi.mock(\"@testing-library/react\", async (original) => {\n  const real = await original<typeof import(\"@testing-library/react\")>();\n  const { createElement, Fragment } = await import(\"react\");\n  const { TooltipProvider } = await import(\"@/components/ui/tooltip\");\n  const { Toaster } = await import(\"@/components/ui/sonner\");\n  const wrapper = ({ children }: { children: unknown }) => createElement(TooltipProvider, null, createElement(Fragment, null, children as never, createElement(Toaster)));\n  return { ...real, render: (ui: never, options?: object) => real.render(ui, { wrapper: wrapper as never, ...options }) };\n});\n\nif (typeof window !== \"undefined\") {\n  const noop = () => undefined;\n  class Observer { observe = noop; unobserve = noop; disconnect = noop; takeRecords = () => []; }\n  const w = window as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;\n  w.ResizeObserver ??= Observer;\n  w.IntersectionObserver ??= Observer;\n  w.matchMedia ??= (query: string) => ({ matches: false, media: query, onchange: null, addEventListener: noop, removeEventListener: noop, addListener: noop, removeListener: noop, dispatchEvent: () => false });\n  const el = Element.prototype as unknown as Record<string, unknown>;\n  el.scrollIntoView ??= noop;\n  el.hasPointerCapture ??= () => false;\n  el.setPointerCapture ??= noop;\n  el.releasePointerCapture ??= noop;\n}\n\nafterEach(async () => {\n  resetNav();\n  if (typeof document !== \"undefined\") (await import(\"@testing-library/react\")).cleanup();\n});\n\nEach screen's code is components/screens/<screen id in lower case>/container.tsx: s-1, s-2, s-3, s-4, s-5, s-6, s-7, s-8, s-9, s-10, s-11, s-12.\n\nlib/api/client.msw.ts exports these API test handlers and example answers (generated from the contract; use them, don't write your own): getGetOverviewResponseMock, getGetImportBatchesResponseMock, getPreviewImportResponseMock, getSynchronizeImportsResponseMock, getGetLotsResponseMock, getMapPurchaseOrderLineResponseMock, getUpdateLotAssumptionsResponseMock, getGetLotForecastResponseMock, getGetInventoryItemsResponseMock, getRecordInventoryMovementResponseMock, getRecordActualWastageResponseMock, getUpdateInventorySettingsResponseMock, getGetInventoryOutlookResponseMock, getGetRationsResponseMock, getCreateRationResponseMock, getOptimizeRationResponseMock, getSelectRationCandidateResponseMock, getGetFeedingPlansResponseMock, getGenerateFeedingPlanResponseMock, getGetFeedingActivitiesResponseMock, getRecordFeedingActivityResponseMock, getGetWeightReadingsResponseMock, getCreateWeightReadingResponseMock, getUpdateWeightReadingResponseMock, getDeleteWeightReadingResponseMock, getGetLotPerformanceResponseMock, getGetOperationalReportsResponseMock, getGetFeedToGainReportResponseMock, getGetProjectDemoResponseMock, getLoadDemoDataResponseMock, getGetOverviewMockHandler, getGetImportBatchesMockHandler, getPreviewImportMockHandler, getSynchronizeImportsMockHandler, getGetLotsMockHandler, getMapPurchaseOrderLineMockHandler, getUpdateLotAssumptionsMockHandler, getGetLotForecastMockHandler, getGetInventoryItemsMockHandler, getRecordInventoryMovementMockHandler, getRecordActualWastageMockHandler, getUpdateInventorySettingsMockHandler, getGetInventoryOutlookMockHandler, getGetRationsMockHandler, getCreateRationMockHandler, getOptimizeRationMockHandler, getSelectRationCandidateMockHandler, getGetFeedingPlansMockHandler, getGenerateFeedingPlanMockHandler, getGetFeedingActivitiesMockHandler, getRecordFeedingActivityMockHandler, getGetWeightReadingsMockHandler, getCreateWeightReadingMockHandler, getUpdateWeightReadingMockHandler, getDeleteWeightReadingMockHandler, getGetLotPerformanceMockHandler, getGetOperationalReportsMockHandler, getGetFeedToGainReportMockHandler, getGetProjectDemoMockHandler, getLoadDemoDataMockHandler, getFeedlotOperationsAPIMock.\n\n<artifact id=\"acs\" kind=\"acceptance-criteria\" sha=\"\">\n[\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"The mock procurement and receiving source contains purchase-order and receiving records with available field values.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays each returned record's source identifier, vendor, cost, head count, weight, breed, age, source, and row status, as available.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the import preview or synchronization results.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-1\",\n  \"given\": \"A synchronization response contains added, duplicate or already-present, and failed row outcomes.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-1.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays separate added, duplicate or already-present, and failed row counts, and displays the source row and reported failure reason for each failed row.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the synchronization results.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"given\": \"A purchase-order line is mapped to multiple lots and the lots are assigned to pens.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-2.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the source purchase-order line, each linked lot, each assigned pen, and the returned pen capacity and current animal count where available.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user reviews the mapping details and pen view.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"given\": \"An import response identifies a duplicate, unmatched record, or row-level problem, or a mapping response refuses an over-capacity assignment.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-2.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the affected source row, its returned status and problem detail, and the capacity refusal and limit where applicable; pending records are absent from pen-operation selectors and a refused assignment is not shown as completed.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user reviews import or mapping results, or attempts the assignment.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-2\",\n  \"given\": \"A purchase-order or receiving record has already been imported using its upstream identifier.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-2.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the duplicate outcome and does not display a second imported record for that identifier.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user synchronizes the same records again.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"given\": \"A lot has incoming animal data, a target weight, and a current measured ADG.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-3.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned target weight, projected market date, and measured gain-rate basis.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the lot forecast.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"given\": \"A lot has no current measured ADG and has a configured default gain rate and target weight.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-3.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the retained submitted assumption values and the forecast response associated with those values, using the configured default gain rate.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the forecast and edits either assumption before requesting a refreshed forecast.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-3\",\n  \"given\": \"The forecast response contains a feeding adjustment or sale-timing recommendation.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-3.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned recommendation and any associated ADG, projected market date, or plan-variance values supplied with it.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user views the lot forecast or insights.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"given\": \"Inventory responses contain feed items and stock movements.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-4.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays each item's name, category, available quantity, unit, unit cost, dry matter percentage, energy value, storage location, and opening, received, consumed, wastage-entered, and closing stock values as available.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens inventory details and movement history.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-4\",\n  \"given\": \"A user enters actual wastage as a stock movement.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-4.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned actual-wastage movement separately from consumption estimates and the configured wastage ratio.\",\n  \"when\": \"The movement is saved and the user opens the movement history.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-5\",\n  \"given\": \"A user has configured a wastage ratio and the inventory response contains projected balances.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-5.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, bi-annual, and annual projected balances reflecting the selected ratio in consumption estimates, with each horizon labeled and no low-stock alert shown.\",\n  \"when\": \"The user selects each supported forecast horizon.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-6\",\n  \"given\": \"A user creates or opens a ration with ingredient and animal-range data.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-6.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the ration name and type, ingredient names and proportions, dry matter percentage, energy value, intended animal type or weight range, and calculated cost per pound.\",\n  \"when\": \"The ration screen is displayed.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"given\": \"Ingredients have recorded costs and nutrition values and a user submits nutritional targets or constraints.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-7.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays returned feasible mixes with ingredient proportions, recorded nutrition values, and costs in lowest-cost order.\",\n  \"when\": \"The user requests ration optimization.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-7\",\n  \"given\": \"An optimization suggestion is displayed and the user has not selected it.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-7.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen shows the suggestion as unapplied; after selection, the screen displays the selected ration for use.\",\n  \"when\": \"The user views the ration workflow.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"given\": \"A pen has an animal count and a user selects a date, ration, and daily bunk rate.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-8.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned plan's date, pen, assigned lot where applicable, animal count, ration, planned quantity, planned feeding time, quantity per animal, total feed cost, expected energy intake, and status.\",\n  \"when\": \"The user generates and opens a feeding plan.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"given\": \"A feeding plan exists for a pen and date.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-8.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned status as Planned, In progress, Completed, or Skipped.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens that plan.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-8\",\n  \"given\": \"A saved plan has actual intake data available.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-8.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays planned and actual feed quantities, the variance, and the updated plan value returned after adjustment.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user reviews the plan and adjusts it using the displayed intake.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"given\": \"A user records a feeding activity for a pen and date where a plan exists.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-9.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned plan link, planned and actual quantities, actual-minus-planned variance, feeding status, feed cost, energy value, and inventory consumed.\",\n  \"when\": \"The activity is saved.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"given\": \"A user records a feeding activity for a pen and date with no plan.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-9.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned activity as unplanned and does not require a plan link.\",\n  \"when\": \"The activity is saved.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-9\",\n  \"given\": \"A pen contains multiple lots.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-9.3\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen requires a separate quantity for each affected lot and displays the returned lot-level quantities.\",\n  \"when\": \"The user enters actual quantities for an activity affecting those lots.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"given\": \"A feeding activity is saved successfully and its response contains planned and actual quantities, cost, energy, and inventory consumption.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-10.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned feed cost, energy, ingredient consumption, affected lot consumption, and actual-minus-planned variance where a plan exists.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user opens the activity details and affected inventory or lot views.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-10\",\n  \"given\": \"The API refuses an activity because available inventory is insufficient.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-10.2\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned shortage error and does not show the activity as saved.\",\n  \"when\": \"A user submits the activity.\"\n },\n {\n  \"req\": \"REQ-11\",\n  \"given\": \"A user records a weight measurement for a lot.\",\n  \"id\": \"AC-11.1\",\n  \"level\": \"ui\",\n  \"then\": \"The screen displays the returned measurement date, number of animals weighed, average animal weight, total lot weight, weight gain since the prior measurement, and notes.\",\n  \"when\": \"The measurement is saved.\"\n },\n {\n  